\relax 
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The design is float throughout, but not all continuous: distinct values per column in each 2000-session window (median over the 147 windows) --- what MDI's cardinality bias keys on.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Permutation importance P2 at the series level, all features (the change in the tail loss when a series is replaced with rows drawn from the refit's training window; mean over the refits, 95\% interval over refits). Columns: \texttt  {har\_ma\_*}'s own value ($\times 10^{3}$); of the other series, those whose interval excludes zero, those whose lower bound clears the upper bound of a pure-noise probe column (trees, QLIKE); their summed importance and the largest single series, both as \% of \texttt  {har\_ma\_*}'s.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Spearman rank correlation, over the non-constant columns, between a column's importance and its number of distinct values. MDI and TreeSHAP track cardinality; permutation importance does not.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Noise probes: on every third refit a second fit appends three pure-noise columns (continuous; 20 values; binary). Median rank among all columns (1 = most important) and, in brackets, the share of the real columns the model uses that rank below the probe; permutation P2 of the continuous probe ($\Delta $QLIKE $\times 10^{3}$, 95\% interval) against \texttt  {har\_ma\_*}'s.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces All-features design, series level: top three per model and measure (share for MDI / $|\beta \cdot sd|$ / SHAP; change as \% of the model's tail loss for permutation and drop-column).}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces All features: ranked bars per measure (series level; top 8). Rows: models; columns: the built-in measure (MDI / gain for the trees, $|\beta \cdot sd|$ for the linear models), permutation P2 $\Delta $MSE (trees) / drop-column $\Delta $MSE (linear), permutation P2 on the tail ($\Delta $QLIKE for the trees, $\Delta $MSE for the linear models), SHAP. Bars with 95\% intervals over refits. Linear permutation / drop-column in $\Delta $MSE (see Section 6).}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Within a tree model: Spearman between measures (series / column level).}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Within a linear model: Spearman between measures (permutation and drop-column in squared error).}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Across models, same measure: range of Spearman over tree--tree pairs, tree--linear pairs, and ridge--lasso (QLIKE permutation: trees only).}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces All features: rank of every series under every model $\times $ measure (1 = most important); measures: MDI / $|\beta \cdot sd|$, permutation P2 ($\Delta $QLIKE trees only, $\Delta $MSE), drop-column (linear), SHAP.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Top-5 stability (series level): share of the 147 refits in which \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} is in the refit's top 5; number of series in the top 5 in at least half the refits; number ever in the top 5. Measures: built-in (MDI or $|\beta sd|$), permutation P2 ($\Delta $QLIKE trees, $\Delta $MSE linear), SHAP.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Regimes: Spearman between the ranking within a stratum and the full-sample ranking (series level): median (minimum) over the calendar years (2018: 13, 2019: 26, 2020: 25, 2021: 25, 2022: 25, 2023: 25, 2024: 8 refits) and over the causal VIX terciles (VIX at 15:30 against the 1/3, 2/3 quantiles of the previous 2000 sessions' 15:30 VIX: low 28, mid 38, high 81 refits); years in which the full-sample leader also leads.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Causally tuned trees (their own extracts: gain per refit and TreeSHAP rows at 16:00): \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} share tuned (untuned), rank correlation tuned vs untuned over the series, and the next three series by tuned SHAP. Permutation was not computed for the tuned trees (their per-refit configurations would have to be refitted).}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Importance over time, all features, series level (a series = the sum of its columns), one column per tree: LightGBM (lgbm\_opt\_00), XGBoost (xgb\_opt\_00), random forest (trial 255). Rows 1--2: each series' share of the refit's total importance, SHAP mean $|\phi |$ on the refit's held-out tail and MDI / gain of the fitted model; rows 3--4: the same series' rank among the 49 series (1 = most important; ranks beyond 20 drawn at 20). Lines: \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} and the next 5 series by overall share (mean over the refits and the three trees), the rest summed as `other'. x axis: the start date of each of the 147 refits' tails. Thin lines: one point per refit --- the trees are refit every 10 sessions, and each point is that refit's model on its own held-out tail (the $\le 10$ sessions it forecasts before the next refit): SHAP averaged over those tail rows, MDI / gain of the fitted model; thick lines: 5-refit centred rolling mean. Grey band: the 2020 crash tails (refits 42--43, tails starting 2020-02-28 and 2020-03-13). Mean \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} share and rank-1 count: LightGBM 44\% SHAP / 52\% MDI, rank 1 in 146 / 147 of 147 refits; XGBoost 47\% SHAP / 54\% MDI, rank 1 in 146 / 147 of 147 refits; random forest 61\% SHAP / 59\% MDI, rank 1 in 147 / 147 of 147 refits. SHAP and MDI shares are split credit: \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} shares its credit with every input correlated with the HAR ladder, and MDI and SHAP inflate continuous, many-valued columns (the noise-probe and cardinality checks above); the shares below \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} are therefore not a ranked feature list.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The same for the per-bar linear models (ridge and lasso, as in Setup), one column per model. Rows 1--2: each series' share of the refit's SHAP mean $|\phi |$ ($\phi _j=\beta _j(x_j-\bar  x_j)$, window mean as background) on the refit's held-out tail, and of $|\beta \cdot sd|$ (coefficient times the column's standard deviation in the refit's 2000-session window, summed over a series' columns); rows 3--4: ranks among the 49 series. The same 147 refit points and tails as the trees (one point per 10 sessions), the same smoothing and grey crash band. Mean \texttt  {har\_ma\_*} share and rank-1 count: ridge 15\% SHAP / 11\% $|\beta \cdot sd|$, rank 1 in 132 / 147 of 147 refits; lasso 46\% SHAP / 46\% $|\beta \cdot sd|$, rank 1 in 137 / 147 of 147 refits. A linear model's credit is split across correlated columns differently from a tree's (the penalty spreads or concentrates it), so the shares compare over time within one model, not across the two figures.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
